{"causes":["The Byzantine Empire's strategic location facilitated the exchange of knowledge between East and West during this period.","The patronage of John II Komnenos supported the creation of the Suda and other scholarly works.","The Suda was influenced by earlier Greek encyclopedias, such as the Etymologicum Magnum."],"effects":["It shaped Byzantine literature and culture, influencing later writers like John Skylitzes.","Its transmission of knowledge helped preserve ancient texts during a time when many were lost or destroyed.","The Suda's emphasis on mythological and historical content contributed to the development of Byzantine historiography."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Byzantine Empire","sourceName":"Byzantine Empire","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"As the Byzantine Empire continued to thrive, it provided a stable environment for scholars like John Tzetzes to create and disseminate their works."},{"sourceSlug":"Etymologicum Magnum","sourceName":"Etymologicum Magnum","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"The Suda drew upon earlier Greek encyclopedias, such as the Etymologicum Magnum, to create its own comprehensive knowledge base."},{"sourceSlug":"John II Komnenos","sourceName":"John II Komnenos","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"The patronage of John II Komnenos enabled the creation and dissemination of scholarly works like the Suda."},{"sourceSlug":"John Skylitzes","sourceName":"John Skylitzes","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"The Suda's emphasis on mythological and historical content contributed to the development of Byzantine historiography, influencing later writers like John Skylitzes."},{"sourceSlug":"Ancient Greece","sourceName":"Ancient Greece","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"The Suda drew upon ancient Greek knowledge, including mythology and historical accounts."},{"sourceSlug":"Hesychasm","sourceName":"Hesychasm","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"The Suda's focus on spiritual and mystical themes reflects the influence of Hesychasm, a Christian mysticism that emerged during this period."},{"sourceSlug":"Byzantine linguistics","sourceName":"Byzantine linguistics","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"The Suda is a valuable resource for scholars studying Byzantine linguistics, providing insight into the development of the Byzantine language."},{"sourceSlug":"John Tzetzes","sourceName":"John Tzetzes","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"As a prominent scholar and contributor to the Suda, John Tzetzes played a key role in shaping its content."},{"sourceSlug":"John Skylitzes' Historia","sourceName":"John Skylitzes' Historia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Suda","targetName":"Suda","context":"The Suda's emphasis on historiography was influenced by John Skylitzes' Historia, a comprehensive historical work that shaped Byzantine historiography."}],"places":[{"name":"Constantinople","role":"Major center of learning"},{"name":"Thessalonica","role":"Important intellectual hub"},{"name":"Alexandria","role":"Influence from ancient Greek scholarship"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Suda","type":"Reference article","slug":"suda"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q216299","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suda"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Suda contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Suda (Greek: Σοῦδα, also written Suidas, though the latter name is a misreading of the title — suda may derive from the Byzantine Greek word for 'fortress' or 'stronghold', σοῦδα) is a massive 10th-century Byzantine Greek encyclopaedia, composed c. 970–975 CE under the reign of Emperor John I Tzimiskes (r. 969–976 CE) or possibly under Basil II (r. 976–1025 CE). It contains approximately 30,000 entries arranged alphabetically, covering a vast range of subjects: lexicography (word definitions, grammatical notes, etymologies), biography (entries on hundreds of ancient and Byzantine authors, rulers, and historical figures), literary criticism (quotations from lost works, assessments of authors' styles), historical summaries, mythological notes, and theological discussions. The Suda is the largest and most comprehensive Byzantine encyclopaedia and one of the most important reference works for Greek antiquity.

The Suda's entries draw on an extraordinary range of earlier sources — many now lost — including lexica (the Hesychius lexicon, the Etymologicum Magnum), historical collections (the excerpts from ancient historians compiled under Constantine VII Porphyrogennetos), biographies (the Onomatologos of Hesychius of Miletus), and a wide range of patristic and classical literature. The Suda's biographical entries — on ancient Greek poets, philosophers, historians, orators, and later Byzantine figures — are often the sole surviving source of information about authors whose works are lost, and its quotations preserve fragments of ancient texts (tragedies, comedies, poetry, prose) that would otherwise be completely unknown.

The Suda is a crucial tool for classical scholars because of its preservation of otherwise-lost information about ancient Greek literature and history. While its compilation is uneven — some entries are detailed and accurate, others are brief or confused — the Suda's role as a repository of the accumulated learning of the Byzantine scholarly tradition makes it an indispensable reference work. The online Suda On Line (SOL) project has produced an open-access English translation of the complete Suda, making it accessible to non-specialist readers for the first time.","subjects":["Byzantine Literature","Medieval Era","Greek Language","Encyclopaedia","Byzantine Culture","Classical Scholarship","Lexicography","Greek Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Suda played a crucial role in preserving ancient texts and transmitting knowledge from the classical period to the Byzantine era, shaping Byzantine literature and culture.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
